      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Single-phase fcc FeCoNi and its relevant single-phase high-entropy-alloy derivatives have coercive fields no greater than 100 Oe.",
      "field": "material_initial_state",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "A FeCoNi-derived high-entropy alloy can retain a single-phase fcc structure.",
      "field": "material_initial_state",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC8840145::web::002::1_introduction"
      ],
      "same_system": "no"
    },
    {
      "text": "The proposed intervention is addition of 0.5–2 at.% carbon to FeCoNi.",
      "field": "intervention",
      "needs_support": false,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Added carbon will occupy interstitial sites in the fcc FeCoNi lattice.",
      "field": "structural_mediator",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC7725351::web::000::abstract"
      ],
      "same_system": "no"
    },
    {
      "text": "Adding 0.5–2 at.% interstitial carbon will produce a controlled lattice distortion in fcc FeCoNi.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The carbon-induced lattice distortion may include a tetragonal symmetry break.",
      "field": "structural_mediator",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The proposed carbon range can preserve a single-phase fcc FeCoNi structure without phase separation.",
      "field": "conditions",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Carbon addition can promote carbide formation or other microstructural and phase changes in an initially single-phase fcc high-entropy alloy.",
      "field": "conditions",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC7725351::web::000::abstract"
      ],
      "same_system": "no"
    },
    {
      "text": "The interstitial-carbon solubility limit in fcc FeCoNi lies within or above the proposed 0.5–2 at.% range.",
      "field": "boundary",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Carbon-induced distortion of the cubic lattice will enhance magnetocrystalline anisotropy.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Enhanced magnetocrystalline anisotropy will increase the energy barrier for domain-wall movement.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The increased barrier to domain-wall movement will increase the coercive field.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Carbon-modified single-phase fcc FeCoNi will have a room-temperature coercive field greater than 100 Oe under matched measurement conditions.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The carbon-modified alloy will largely preserve the high saturation magnetization of fcc FeCoNi.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The hypothesis can be tested by comparing undoped and carbon-doped samples made with otherwise identical synthesis and annealing procedures.",
      "field": "falsification_experiment.design",
